{"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/OpenUSD"]]},"seeAlsoSections":[{"generated":true,"anchor":"Using-SwiftUsd-from-C++","identifiers":["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxInteropMemorySafety","doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxMacros"],"title":"Using SwiftUsd from C++"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Getting Started","anchor":"Getting-Started","type":"heading","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Before you can start using SwiftUsd, you need to add it as a dependency to your Xcode project or Swift Package, and then configure a few build settings."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","name":"Note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Despite the fact that SwiftUsd is a Swift Package, you can use it in a pure-C++ Xcode project\/target, as well as in a pure-C++ Swift Package target.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside"},{"style":"important","name":"Important","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swift Package targets cannot mix Swift and C++ code in a single target. "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/swiftlang\/swift-package-manager\/issues\/8945","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To do so, follow the steps in "},{"overridingTitle":"Getting Started for Swift","type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/GettingStarted#Getting-Started","isActive":true,"overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"text":"Getting Started for Swift","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":". If you’re using a pure-C++ target, you don’t need to change the "},{"code":"C++ and Objective-C Interoperability","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" mode in Xcode or add "},{"code":".interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" to your target’s "},{"code":"swiftSettings","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":